---
import { GITHUB, ISSUES, RELEASES } from "../data";
import Section from "./Section.astro";

const card = "panel px-5 pt-1 pb-2 [&_h3]:mt-4";
---

<Section id="warning" title={'"Windows protected your PC"'}>
  <p>
    You will probably see this the first time you open Ravenpost. It doesn't mean Windows found anything wrong with
    it. It means the file isn't <em>code-signed</em>: signing needs a paid certificate tied to a company or a verified
    person, and a new app without one has no reputation with Microsoft yet. Ravenpost is made by one person in their
    spare time, and the certificate isn't there yet.
  </p>

  <div class="my-5 grid gap-4">
    <div class={card}>
      <h3>Windows: SmartScreen</h3>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-5">
        <li>
          If your browser says the file isn't commonly downloaded, keep it. In Edge: the <strong>...</strong> menu next
          to the download, <strong>Keep</strong>, <strong>Show more</strong>, <strong>Keep anyway</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>Open it. When Windows warns you, click <strong>More info</strong>.</li>
        <li>Click <strong>Run anyway</strong>.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Windows remembers, and won't ask again for this file.</p>
    </div>
    <div class={card}>
      <h3>Windows 11: Smart App Control</h3>
      <p>
        If Smart App Control is on, it may block the file outright, with no "Run anyway". It judges each file through
        Microsoft's cloud, and an unsigned file it hasn't seen before can be refused.
      </p>
      <p>
        Either <a href={ISSUES}>open an issue</a> and say which file was blocked, so a new build can be made, or
        turn Smart App Control off in Windows Security, under App &amp; browser control. Think twice about the second: it
        protects you from other apps too, and on some versions of Windows it can't be turned back on without
        reinstalling Windows.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class={card}>
      <h3>macOS</h3>
      <p>Open the zip and move Ravenpost to your Applications folder first, then open it from there.</p>
      <p>
        macOS says it can't check Ravenpost for malicious software. Close the message, open <strong
          >System Settings</strong
        > (System Preferences before macOS 13), then <strong>Privacy &amp; Security</strong>, scroll down and click <strong
          >Open Anyway</strong
        > next to Ravenpost. On macOS 14 or older, right-clicking the app and choosing <strong>Open</strong> does the same.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Check it yourself</h3>
  <p>
    Ravenpost's whole source is <a href={GITHUB}>on GitHub</a>, MIT licensed. Every download on this page is built by
    GitHub Actions from that source, in public, and attached to the release by the same run. You can read what each
    release changed on <a href={RELEASES}>its release page</a>, or build it yourself.
  </p>
</Section>
